A||||5||WisconsinGT||88GT Brake help||||1
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||10-02-1999||01:03 AM||||I am going to repair/replace my brake system in the next couple of weeks. Right now my brake pedal will go close to the floor while pressed. (Thinking I need a new Master cyilinder) Also, when I come to a stop, I can feel a warping feeling. (Feels like it catches every other turn.) Finally, when I depress the brake pedal, I hear a clicking sound that slows as I come to a stop. Oh yeah, if I run my finger over the rotors, they are not smooth.<P>Could y'all give me any advice on what I can do to my brakes to overhaul them? i.e. Buy new stock brakes? Get any after market brakes parts? My budget for this is $500.00. That has to include labor as well. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||88 Fiero||10-02-1999||01:11 AM||||Wisconsin...First check and see if your Calipers were bleeding...if so either rebuild them or replace them...also since your going to do that..you might as well replace your brake lines with braided ones...They do wonders when you step on the brakes..plus the factory brake lines they do expand when you step on the brakes... as far as the rotors replace them too specially if the are warp... you can order the cross drilled ones..from KVR or Fiero Store..I think Fiero Store sells this Cross Drilled Rotors very expensive...specially the '88 Fiero. Majority of the guys at my Fiero Club have switch to braided brake lines and we love it tho..and that's a good investment...<p>[This message has been edited by 88 Fiero (edited 10-02-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||10-02-1999||01:56 AM||||The pulsation you detect may also be some tread separation or a tire out of round.  The 88 front brake system is slightly more expensive than previous years, but very easy to rebuild. Stock replacement rotors run about $50 each, so turning the original ones may be preferable if they are not warped.  Pay close attention to the condition of the caliper sliders, they seem to be a problem area on the 88's. Lubrication of the sliders and bores with high temp silicone grease is a must.  Using brake anti-squeal (the blue permatex junk) on the pads during reassembly seems to help alot.  The clicking sound is a little strange, you may want to ensure that the calipers are not loose, and check the condition of the front wheel bearings.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pretender||10-04-1999||01:06 AM||||"Trek Auto" quoted me $90+ for each front rotor for my '88! So i just went with the cross drilled rotors from the fiero store, they seem to help out very good considering that from the sound of your problem mine was way worse! They also look damn good and last way longer then the "stock" rotors, carbo pads were also installed on the front and i think my total bill was like $250 but i installed them myself and believe me, anyone can do this!!!!! Or if you live near me i'd be happy to assist!..L8r.||||reg||10
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||10-04-1999||01:22 AM||||Thanks Pretender! Where are you at? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||JIM GOOSE||10-06-1999||02:10 PM||||Why buy new rotors if yours only need to be turned? MUCH cheaper as long as thickness is OK.||||reg||1
